{%- comment -%}
  The highest quantity a cart line may actually be set to. Outputs a bare
  number, or nothing at all when the line is genuinely uncapped.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  Both cart surfaces rendered `max` from `variant.quantity_rule.max` alone —
  the merchant's per-order limit, which is null on every product in this
  store. So the input had no ceiling, the stepper's + never disabled, and
  asking for more than exists went all the way to the server, which answered
  422 "Only 1 item was added to your cart due to availability" and left the
  number the shopper typed sitting in a box the cart had already refused.

  Stock is knowable here, so the ceiling belongs here: prevention instead of
  an apology after a round-trip.

  WHAT COUNTS AS A CEILING
    · the merchant's order limit, when one is set
    · available stock, but ONLY when Shopify is tracking it and the variant
      is not set to continue selling when sold out — the same two-part test
      the buy module uses for its scarcity dots. A variant that oversells on
      purpose has no ceiling, and saying otherwise would block a sale the
      merchant wants to make.
  The lower of the two wins when both apply.

  NEVER BELOW WHAT IS ALREADY IN THE BAG
  Stock can fall after a line is added — someone else buys the last one, or
  the merchant corrects a count. Emitting a max under the current quantity
  would mark a line the shopper already holds as invalid and make the browser
  fight them over a number they cannot change. The floor is the line's own
  quantity: no increase is possible (the stepper's + disables itself at max),
  and nothing already in the bag is called an error.

  Accepts:
  - item: {Object} a cart line item, required

  Usage:
  {%- capture q_max -%}{%- render 'cart-quantity-max', item: item -%}{%- endcapture -%}
  {%- assign q_max = q_max | strip -%}
  {%- if q_max != blank %} max="{{ q_max }}"{% endif -%}
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign qm_ceiling = nil

  assign qm_rule = item.variant.quantity_rule.max
  if qm_rule != blank and qm_rule != null
    assign qm_ceiling = qm_rule
  endif

  if item.variant.inventory_management == 'shopify' and item.variant.inventory_policy != 'continue'
    comment
      A sold-out or oversold variant reports 0 or a negative number. Clamped
      to 0 so the comparison below is against a real floor rather than a
      negative one; the line's own quantity then lifts it to something sane.
    endcomment
    assign qm_stock = item.variant.inventory_quantity | at_least: 0
    if qm_ceiling == nil or qm_stock < qm_ceiling
      assign qm_ceiling = qm_stock
    endif
  endif

  if qm_ceiling != nil
    assign qm_ceiling = qm_ceiling | at_least: item.quantity
  endif
-%}
{%- if qm_ceiling != nil -%}{{ qm_ceiling }}{%- endif -%}
